WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.830
This battle scene in
"Spider-Man: Far From Home"

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.110
wouldn't have been possible
without "Harry Potter,"

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.950
"The Matrix," or even "James Bond."

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.490
The midway fight sequence
called the "illusion battle"

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.970
was partly inspired by these movies.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.470
Although the movie's visual
effects were completed

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.850
by a combination of companies,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.253
Framestore was responsible
for this thrilling section.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.930
Visual effects supervisor Alexis Wajsbrot,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.870
who previously worked on "Thor: Ragnarok"

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.673
and "Doctor Strange," explains
to us how it was done.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.370
And, spoiler alert, we're going to be

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.793
revealing details about the film.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.720
In this sequence, Spider-Man visits

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.240
what he believes to be the
Berlin office of Europol,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.470
but instead is a trap set by Mysterio.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.210
The villain uses holographic techniques

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.450
and projector drones to create

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.200
a sequence of horrifying illusions.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.906
Spider-Man: I know this isn't real.

00:01:18.906 --> 00:01:21.060
Mysterio: Do you, though?

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.080
Alexis Wajsbrot: Usually, the process is,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.542
there is a script, and then
there is a production period

00:01:27.542 --> 00:01:30.418
where they shoot what the script is.

00:01:30.418 --> 00:01:32.770
And then we are adding visual effects

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.090
on top in post-production.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.025
But on the case of this one,

00:01:37.025 --> 00:01:39.350
the sequence was not
locked at script stage.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.920
We were at concepting at the same time

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.830
as we were animating shots.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.860
Narrator: To make a sequence like this,

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.650
the shots must go
through different stages,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.950
with 120 employees all working in unison,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.940
modeling, texturing, shading, rigging,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.163
animating, rendering,
and lighting the shot.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.290
The movie's star, Tom Holland,

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.390
has his movements recorded
in a motion-capture suit,

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:13.053
which is then passed to the animators.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.150
To begin, the Framestore art department

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.170
worked on creative references
with director Jon Watts

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:24.283
and VFX supervisor Janek Sirrs.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.660
Visual references included the comic books

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.450
as well as Surrealist paintings.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.673
Wajsbrot: References on YouTube
of cool videos that we find.

00:02:34.673 --> 00:02:38.090
There were one where a
drone was emitting lights.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.830
We watched "The Matrix"
for having a lot of Agent Smith's,

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.620
for our sequence where
there is a lot of spider-men

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.410
jumping onto the main Spidey.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:47.900
Of course, we looked at the "James Bond"

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.970
title sequence for "Spectre."

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.690
We used that reference for a sequence

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.800
in the graveyard with zombie Iron Man.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.070
With Marvel, we are
doing a lot more in CG,

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:01.201
a lot more completely computer-generated,

00:03:01.201 --> 00:03:03.000
whereas in "James Bond" they shot

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.890
a lot more elements, and it's more 2D.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.900
Narrator: The team also used a scene

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.620
from "Harry Potter and
the Order of the Phoenix"

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.440
as a reference, when
orbs fall on the gang.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.873
Wajsbrot: We wanted the
globes to be actually

00:03:15.873 --> 00:03:18.890
Mysterio globe, Mysterio helmet, to fall,

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.350
which didn't end up in the movie.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.750
We even looked at "Indiana Jones,"

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:26.583
having a giant globe of Mysterio
head chasing Spider-Man.

00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:29.217
"The Evil Dead" for the mirror sequence.

00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:31.003
Ash: I don't think so.

00:03:31.003 --> 00:03:33.250
Narrator: Once the references were locked,

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:38.190
the team moved to
storyboard, then concept art.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:43.190
In total, 150 pieces of
concept art were produced.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.113
These were turned into animations.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.960
The transitions from one scene to another

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.260
were particularly difficult.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:57.260
They used an FX-driven transition
dubbed the BARF effect,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.200
or binarily augmented retro-framing,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.500
which is based on the
transition used by Iron Man

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.968
in a scene from "Captain
America: Civil War."

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:10.161
Wajsbrot: What we call
references a blue twinkly effect,

00:04:10.161 --> 00:04:12.120
which is a lot of blue pixels.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.100
What is it that we can make it better?

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.100
So we transformed this
2D pixel into 3D voxels

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.320
and make the effect a bit more 3D.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.170
When you transition from one
environment to the other,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.320
you need the two environments
to have some similarities.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.310
So, for example, at the
beginning we are transitioning

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.500
from Europol to actually
a construction site.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.330
That's where we completely transformed

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.150
the construction site in full CG,

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.280
we completely rebuild it to
make sense of the geography.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.896
So there is always a link.

00:04:42.896 --> 00:04:44.362
When you are somewhere in Europol,

00:04:44.362 --> 00:04:46.510
you are somewhere in
the construction site.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.300
Narrator: The transitions
were also inspired

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.580
by "The Truman Show," namely the idea

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.500
of walking into a landscape
that turns out to be

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.313
a different texture, such
as a wall or a floor.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.350
FX such as Mysterio's blast

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.680
or the smoke inside his helmet

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.073
were created by other
vendors, such as Scanline.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.450
Framestore created the FX of green smoke

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.743
and applied it to different environments.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.820
The texture of the smoke can be dry ice,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.743
wispy, or thick like a floor.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.170
Weather systems were a
difficult effect to achieve.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.200
For this, the team had to work on

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.393
the texture of different effects.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.400
Wajsbrot: So, one that
is actually more complex

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.630
is the snow interaction on Spider-Man

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.600
and the building piercing through.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.460
It's really tricky to have realistic snow;

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.663
it always looks a bit
like jelly or too powdery.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.590
Narrator: The sequence involved

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.490
three main Spider-Man suits:

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:56.013
The homemade suit, as seen
in "Spider-Man: Homecoming,"

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.302
the stealth suit,

00:05:59.302 --> 00:06:02.530
and the homecoming suit.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.630
The scene is lit with
strong directional light,

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.360
which was a challenge for the texture.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.830
Too thin, and the suit starts to show

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.620
too much muscle definition.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.343
Too baggy, and the suit looks
ill-fitting and wrinkly.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.530
At one stage, we see
Spidey in a "shard room,"

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:24.270
where he looks at multiple
versions of himself

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.520
all moving independently.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.590
For this, the team had to create

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.431
20 individual animated Spider-Man suits.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.090
They also had to design the character suit

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.006
for zombie Iron Man.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.610
Wajsbrot: We had some
very, very gory version

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.520
where we still see some bits of skin,

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.320
and muscle, and flesh,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.910
which was a bit too far for Marvel.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.740
We went full skeleton,
and that was not enough.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.570
For us, it's a big challenge to model

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.263
all of these broken pieces of
metal as well as the skull,

00:06:55.263 --> 00:06:59.460
and then making sure that
we shade them correctly.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.353
We also used "Avengers: Age of Ultron"

00:07:02.353 --> 00:07:04.130
as a reference, because there is lots of

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.620
broken robots with hanging cables

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.060
and hanging pieces at the
beginning of the sequence.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.940
We used that as well as a reference

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.440
to see how we can make a zombie Iron Man

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.543
with all of these
hanging pieces of metals.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.480
Narrator: The team had worked on

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.920
similarly trippy sequences

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.340
in "Doctor Strange" and
"Avengers: Endgame."

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.230
Wajsbrot: The previous
Marvel movies actually

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:26.720
what we cannot do.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.830
So, we looked at "Doctor Strange,"

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.790
OK, we cannot do all of that.

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.140
Spider-Man gets sliced into
lots of different pieces

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:37.000
in space, but because they'd
done that in "Endgame" already,

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.000
we couldn't do that.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.620
Narrator: The sequence
took nine months to make,

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.920
from first reference to finished product.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.503
Which was no mean feat, given
its scale and complexity.

